        -: 9905:/*==
        -: 9906:density() and the bw.* bandwidth selectors
        -: 9907:---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        -: 9908:A port of R 4.6.1's stats::density.default(), together with the five bandwidth
        -: 9909:rules its `bw =` string can name, so that a Perl caller gets the same grid, the
        -: 9910:same bandwidth and the same estimate R would give.
        -: 9911:
        -: 9912:Every piece below is named after, and follows line by line, the R source it
        -: 9913:comes from:
        -: 9914:
        -: 9915:  src/library/stats/R/density.R      density.default()
        -: 9916:  src/library/stats/R/bandwidths.R   bw.nrd0 / bw.nrd / bw.SJ / bw.ucv / bw.bcv
        -: 9917:  src/library/stats/src/massdist.c   BinDist()
        -: 9918:  src/library/stats/src/bandwidths.c bw_den / bw_den_binned / bw_phi4 /
        -: 9919:                                     bw_phi6 / bw_ucv / bw_bcv
        -: 9920:  src/library/stats/src/approx.c     approx1()
        -: 9921:  src/library/stats/src/optimize.c   Brent_fmin(), which is R's optimize()
        -: 9922:  src/main/seq.c                     do_seq(), for seq.int(from, to, length.out=)
        -: 9923:  src/library/stats/src/zeroin.c     which is ft_zeroin() above already
        -: 9924:
        -: 9925:R disperses the data onto a grid, convolves it with the discretised kernel
        -: 9926:using fft(), and interpolates back with approx().  It rounds n up to a power of
        -: 9927:two precisely so that transform is cheap, which means the transform length here
        -: 9928:(2n) is always a power of two and a plain radix-2 Cooley-Tukey is all that is
        -: 9929:needed -- see dens_fft().
        -: 9930: ==========================================================================*/
